WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.456
Retrieval is the decision layer that chooses what evidence reaches the model.

00:00:04.456 --> 00:00:09.538
A RAG system usually cannot send the entire knowledge base into the context window.

00:00:09.538 --> 00:00:14.256
It selects a limited set of chunks using query processing, filters, similarity search,

00:00:14.256 --> 00:00:17.523
keyword matching, ranking, reranking, source rules, and context limits.

00:00:17.523 --> 00:00:20.427
Those decisions strongly influence answer quality and safety.

00:00:20.427 --> 00:00:24.420
Retrieval should be constrained by user permissions, tenant scope, workflow purpose,

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.235
source eligibility, and freshness requirements.

00:00:26.235 --> 00:00:30.591
A user should not receive a chunk just because it is semantically

00:00:30.591 --> 00:00:32.769
similar when the source is restricted,

00:00:32.769 --> 00:00:34.947
expired, or outside the approved workflow.

00:00:34.947 --> 00:00:37.487
More retrieved text is not always better.

00:00:37.487 --> 00:00:42.206
Too much context can crowd out important evidence, introduce stale or conflicting material,

00:00:42.206 --> 00:00:45.473
increase cost, and make the answer harder to steer.

00:00:45.473 --> 00:00:49.103
Retrieval design should consider top-k choices, relevance thresholds, duplicate handling,

00:00:49.103 --> 00:00:53.459
source diversity, freshness preferences, and no-answer behavior when the evidence is weak.

00:00:53.459 --> 00:00:56.000
Context assembly is also an architectural boundary.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.630
The final prompt should separate trusted system instructions, application policy,

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:01.444
user requests, and retrieved evidence.

00:01:01.444 --> 00:01:06.526
Retrieved chunks should be labeled as source material rather than treated as system policy.

00:01:06.526 --> 00:01:11.245
That separation helps the model use evidence without confusing it with higher-priority rules.

00:01:11.245 --> 00:01:14.875
A mature private RAG system makes retrieval observable and tunable.

00:01:14.875 --> 00:01:19.957
Engineers should be able to see which chunks were considered, which filters were applied,

00:01:19.957 --> 00:01:21.772
why certain sources were selected,

00:01:21.772 --> 00:01:25.401
and whether the answer was grounded in the retrieved material.

00:01:25.401 --> 00:01:28.305
Retrieval settings should be reviewed as requirements change.

00:01:28.305 --> 00:01:32.661
A workflow that supports exploratory research may need different ranking, source diversity,

00:01:32.661 --> 00:01:37.380
and no-answer behavior than a workflow used for policy guidance or customer-facing support.
